'''''The World of Ice & Fire: The Untold History of Westeros and the Game of Thrones''''' is a companion book for [[George R. R. Martin]]'s ''[[A Song of Ice and Fire]]'' [[fantasy]] series. Written by Martin, [[Elio M. García Jr. and Linda Antonsson]], it was published by [[Bantam Books|Bantam]] on October 28, 2014.<ref name="RS 2014-11">{{cite magazine |url=https://www.rollingstone.com/tv/features/10-craziest-things-we-learned-from-world-of-ice-and-fire-20141103 |title=10 Craziest Things We Learned From ''World of Ice & Fire'' |first=Sean T. |last=Collins |date=November 3, 2014 |magazine=[[Rolling Stone]]|access-date=November 7, 2014}}</ref><ref name="HP 2014-10">{{cite web |url=http://www.huffingtonpost.com/2014/10/27/westeros-george-rr-martin_n_6056720.html |title=Here's What Westeros Really Looks Like, According To George R.R. Martin |work=[[The Huffington Post]] |first=Bill |last=Bradley |date=October 27, 2014 |access-date=November 1, 2014}}</ref><ref name="WSJ 2014-10">{{cite web |url=https://online.wsj.com/articles/george-r-r-martin-writes-a-big-ice-and-fire-footnote-1414679391 |title=George R.R. Martin Writes a Big ''Ice and Fire'' History |work=[[The Wall Street Journal]] |first=Christopher John |last=Farley |date=October 30, 2014 |access-date=November 1, 2014}}</ref> The 326-page volume is a fully illustrated "history compendium" of Martin's fictional [[World of A Song of Ice and Fire|world]], written from the perspective of an in-world "Maester" and featuring newly written material, family trees, and extensive maps and artwork.<ref name="Tor 2014-10-29">{{cite web |url=http://www.tor.com/blogs/2014/10/the-world-of-ice-and-fire-george-rr-martin-book-review |title=19 Strange Things Hiding in ''The World of Ice and Fire'' |first=Chris |last=Lough |date=October 29, 2014 |publisher=[[Tor Books]] |access-date=October 31, 2014}}</ref><ref name="Tor 2014-10-30">{{cite web |url=http://www.tor.com/blogs/2014/10/the-world-of-ice-and-fire-book-art |title=Check Out Dragonstone and Other Art Pieces From ''The World of Ice & Fire'' |date=October 30, 2014 |publisher=Tor Books |access-date=October 31, 2014}}</ref>

==Contents==
[[File:The World of Ice and Fire.JPG|thumb|left|Linda Antonsson, Elio Garcia and George R.R. Martin in a 2015 panel at Archipelacon, [[Mariehamn]]]]
The format of ''The World of Ice & Fire'' is intentionally a replication of a "real history book" in which sources can contradict each other.<ref name="WSJ 2014-10"/> Martin worked closely with the artists to render the characters and locales as he himself imagined them, as opposed to how they may be portrayed in [[HBO]]'s ''[[Game of Thrones]]'' series and other media, such as [[comic book]]s and games.<ref name="HP 2014-10"/>

The book details the fictional history of the Seven Kingdoms, from the pre-historic Dawn Age up to just before the main book series.<ref>{{cite web | url=https://georgerrmartin.com/grrm_book/the-world-of-ice-and-fire/ | title=THE WORLD OF ICE AND FIRE &#124; George R.R. Martin }}</ref>

===Art===
Martin teased the book in July 2013 with a blog post praising Marc Simonetti's drawing of the [[Iron Throne (A Song of Ice and Fire)|Iron Throne]], which was to appear in the book, as very close to his own idea of the throne, compared to the TV series version.<ref name="GRRM 2013-07">{{cite web |url=http://grrm.livejournal.com/327569.html |title=Not A Blog: The Real Iron Throne |first=George R. R. |last=Martin |publisher=GRRM.livejournal.com |date=July 8, 2013 |access-date=November 8, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141225035210/http://grrm.livejournal.com/327569.html |archive-date=December 25, 2014 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Upon the 2014 release of ''The World of Ice & Fire'', he called its representation of the throne by Simonetti "absolutely right."<ref name="BI 2014-10">{{cite web |url=http://www.businessinsider.com/george-rr-martin-no-one-gets-game-of-thrones-iron-throne-right-2014-10 |title=George R.R. Martin: No One Ever Gets The Most Iconic Part Of ''Game Of Thrones'' Right |first=Kirsten |last=Acuna |work=[[Business Insider]] |date=October 28, 2014 |access-date=November 8, 2014}}</ref> Martin said he had wanted the book to be a fully illustrated volume with art from "the top fantasy illustrators in the world."<ref name="92Y Plus full talk with George R.R. Martin"/> 

''The World of Ice & Fire'' features the work of 27 illustrators: Rene Aigner, Ryan Barger, Arthur Bozonnet, José Daniel Cabrera Peña, Jennifer Sol Cai, Thomas Denmark, Jennifer Drummond, Jordi González Escamilla, Michael Gellatly, Tomasz Jedruszek, [[Michael Komarck]], John McCambridge, Mogri, [[Ted Nasmith]], Karla Ortiz, Rahedie Yudha Pradito, Dhian Prasetya, Paolo Puggioni, Jonathan Roberts, Thomas Siagian, Marc Simonetti, Chase Stone, Philip Straub, Justin Sweet, Nutchapol Thitinunthakorn, [[Magali Villeneuve]], and [[Doug Wheatley|Douglas Wheatley]].<ref>{{cite book |first1=George R. R. |last1=Martin |first2=Elio M. |last2=García Jr. |first3=Linda |last3=Antonsson |title=The World of Ice & Fire |date=2014 |chapter=Art Credits |page=326 |publisher=Random House Publishing |isbn=978-0-553-80544-4}}</ref>

==Development==
Elio García and Linda Antonsson head the ''A Song of Ice and Fire'' [[fansite]] [[Westeros.org]].<ref name="Tor 2014-10-29"/> [[George R. R. Martin]] enlisted them in 2006 to assist with the project, which at the time he believed would be finished by 2008.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.georgerrmartin.com/bantam-to-publish-the-world-of-ice-and-fire/ |title=BANTAM TO PUBLISH THE WORLD OF ICE AND FIRE &#124; George R.R. Martin |access-date=2019-08-01 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190712162524/http://www.georgerrmartin.com/bantam-to-publish-the-world-of-ice-and-fire/ |archive-date=2019-07-12 |url-status=dead }}</ref> García is a Martin "superfan" whom the author and HBO have consulted on details previously established by Martin in the series.<ref name="New Yorker">{{cite magazine |last=Miller |first=Laura |url=http://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2011/04/11/just-write-it |title=Just Write It! A fantasy author and his impatient fans. |magazine=[[The New Yorker]] |date=April 11, 2011 |access-date=November 7, 2014}}</ref>

The book's planned length was 500,000 words, but the historical references collected by García and Antonsson from the books amounted to 70,000 words, and after Martin "polished it, expanded it and {{sic|fill}} in the holes," it became 1,000,000 words. Martin also started writing "sidebar" stories for the book, but at one point, he realized he had written 3,500,000 more words. As this did not fit the original concept of a fully illustrated book—the number of illustrations remaining the same—Martin removed his sidebar stories, and the rest was abridged by García and Antonsson. Parts of the removed material appeared in [[Gardner Dozois]]'s anthologies ''[[Dangerous Women (anthology)|Dangerous Women]]'' (as ''[[The Princess and the Queen]]''), ''[[Rogues (anthology)|Rogues]]'' (as ''[[The Rogue Prince]]'') and ''[[The Book of Swords (anthology)|The Book of Swords]]'' (as ''[[The Sons of the Dragon]]'').<ref>{{cite podcast|url=https://soundcloud.com/sfinsfpodcast/august-2018-george-rr-martin-special-event|title=August 2018: George R.R. Martin Special Event|via=soundcloud.com|date=August 14, 2018|access-date=September 18, 2018}}</ref>

Addressing comparisons of ''The World of Ice & Fire'' to [[J. R. R. Tolkien]]'s ''[[The Silmarillion]]'', Martin clarified that while his book provides a basic overview of the many areas of his fictional world and their histories, he planned to someday publish a more extensive volume focusing primarily on the [[Targaryen]]s, which he jokingly dubbed "The GRRMarillion."<ref name="92Y Plus full talk with George R.R. Martin">{{cite interview |last=Martin |first=George R. R. |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Vcy-EhkHXnE#t=117 |title=George R. R. Martin: The World of Ice and Fire (Video) |interviewer=Laura Miller |date=October 27, 2014 |access-date=November 9, 2014 |location=New York}}</ref> As plans for an eventual second companion book became more solidified, Martin said that it would more formally be titled ''[[Fire & Blood (book)|Fire & Blood]]'', because it gives expanded detail on the reign of each Targaryen king.<ref>[https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FLFuAtFbg6w George R. R. Martin on ''The World of Ice and Fire'', "Introductions"]{{dead YouTube link|date=August 2026}}</ref>

